---
import FighterStabilizerLook from "../../../components/studies/FighterStabilizerLook.svelte";
import MovementBench from "../../../components/studies/MovementBench.svelte";
import StabilizerFlight from "../../../components/studies/StabilizerFlight.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "base-movement-ion");
if (!study) throw new Error("Missing base movement study metadata");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section aria-labelledby="problem">
    <h2 id="problem">01 / Momentum worth learning to control</h2>
    <p>
      Start at rest, fire the engines, then release them. How quickly should the
      ship gather speed, how should acceleration change as speed builds, and
      what should happen when thrust stops? These establish the basic experience
      of movement before turning and recovery can be judged. The successful
      alpha gives us a playable foundation; its current tuning is a starting
      point for investigation, not a settled straight-flight design.
    </p>
    <p>
      This study should settle the basic relationship between engine input,
      turning, acceleration and coasting. Two controls must support both a
      beginner's deliberate change of course and a practiced player's flowing
      manoeuvres. Momentum, mass and engine direction should explain what
      happens, while responsiveness and recoverability guide where the game
      simplifies physics. Turning response, braking effort and behaviour at
      speed remain questions to investigate together, rather than independent
      numbers to tune.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ion is the simple default engine. Light supplies the baseline; Heavy
      counts as 1.75 Light engines. That relationship must become meaningful in
      flight; it does not yet assign every mass, energy or speed value the same
      multiplier. Their established bodies and beam effects carry into the
      trials. Gravity and other engine types are excluded. Hull redesign, camera
      redesign and survival-resource balancing are outside this movement
      decision.
    </p>
    <h3>What success looks like</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Easy to start:</strong>
        a new player can understand the two controls and deliberately change
        course.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Movement makes sense:</strong>
        the ship carries momentum, and the player can understand how firing an
        engine changes its path.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mistakes are recoverable:</strong>
        overshooting gives the player something to correct, without a long
        struggle to regain control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Practice pays off:</strong>
        anticipating turns leads to smoother, more accurate flying.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Both ions work:</strong>
        Light feels dependable; Heavy's 1.75-Light contribution is useful and
        understandable without making flight unmanageable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Flying is fun on its own:</strong>
        accelerating, turning and drifting invite another go, even without
        gravity or survival pressure.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Playable trials will test these criteria through changing course and
      recovering from overshoots, with both ion variants. Human play review must
      judge clarity, control and enjoyment; simulation checks can verify the
      underlying rules but cannot establish fun.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first investigation establishes acceleration, speed and coasting with
      Light ion. The shared fourteen-second input sequence also exposes how side
      engines change the path; it does not select a new turning model. Heavy
      remains a later scaling comparison.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / See what sustained thrust does</h2>
    <h3>02.1 / Four paths, one input sequence</h3>
    <p>
      Compare one aft engine, two side engines, and two aft plus two side
      engines against a straight-moving reference at 75% of the chosen maximum
      speed. Each run lasts fourteen seconds, with one second per input: LR, LR,
      coast, L, coast, LR, coast, R, coast, R, coast, LR, LR, LR. The three
      powered ships start at rest at the same origin, facing right. The
      reference starts already moving. Playback starts paused; Play or Replay
      starts the animation.
    </p>
    <MovementBench client:load />
    <p>
      Acceleration means the contribution of one aligned Light engine at rest,
      on a fixed common effective mass. Adding engines adds force without adding
      mass in this fixture. Side thrust uses the real Fighter mount angles and
      turn-response calculation; aft engines fire only when both controls are
      held. The aft-only ship is deliberately artificial: real builds require
      side engines. The constant-speed reference ignores all inputs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bench reuses the production engine impulse: speed-increasing thrust
      tapers toward the chosen powered-speed limit, while braking and transverse
      response remain available. This is the current candidate to inspect, not
      an accepted movement rule. Travel momentum persists on release; rotation
      uses the current direct response. There is no gravity, drag, energy
      depletion, wrapping or randomness. Simulation uses 60 ticks per second
      with four substeps; seeking reads the same precomputed run. Distance is
      accumulated path length, not displacement. Route framing and graph scales
      stay fixed throughout each run and refit when settings are applied.
    </p>
    <p>
      Look for how engine count changes acceleration, how quickly each ship
      approaches the reference speed, and what happens through single-engine
      input and release. These traces expose the rules and their consequences;
      they do not yet establish whether free flight is fun.
    </p>
    <p>
      Review of 02.1 establishes engine direction and summed engine strength as
      the basis for a semi-physical movement model. Relatively high acceleration
      per engine with a low speed ceiling produced the most interesting path
      shapes in human review. No exact parameter pair was recorded; this is a
      promising region to investigate, not final tuning or proof of playable
      handling. How engine strength determines the speed ceiling remains open.
    </p>

    <h3 id="ceiling-comparison">
      02.2 / How much speed should combined force buy?
    </h3>
    <p>
      Combined directional force is the chosen basis for acceleration and the
      powered speed ceiling. Compare the original example ships, plus two aft
      engines to make alignment directly comparable with the side pair. All use
      the same Fighter geometry, effective mass, Light engines and
      fourteen-second sequence from 02.1.
    </p>
    <MovementBench
      comparison
      label="Directional force and speed comparison"
      client:load
    />
    <p>
      During LR, one aft supplies 1 equivalent, two aft supply 2, two angled
      side engines supply about 1.80, and both pairs supply about 3.80. Sideways
      forces cancel in the mirrored pair. One combined force vector drives each
      powered ship; its magnitude determines acceleration and the ceiling. Aft
      engines fire only on LR; side engines follow their respective controls.
      The artificial side-pair reference instantly travels at half its two-side
      LR ceiling while either control is held, following its facing. One button
      turns at half the current Fighter turn rate; LR holds heading. Release
      preserves travel velocity and stops rotation. This replaces the original
      constant-speed reference for turning review.
    </p>
    <p>
      The candidate curve is V(E) = V₁ × [1 + B × (1 − exp(−k × (E − 1)))] for E
      ≥ 1. V₁ is the one-Light ceiling, B the extra-speed allowance, and k the
      falloff rate. B = 0 gives every powered arrangement the same ceiling.
      Larger B gives extra force more speed benefit; larger k reaches that
      allowance with fewer engines. Start with B = 0, then compare 0.4 while
      keeping acceleration and V₁ fixed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solid lines show speed and dashed lines show powered ceilings. A zero
      ceiling means no active force, not braking: coasting preserves velocity,
      and a lower ceiling never clamps existing speed. The graphs and readouts
      distinguish traversed length from position. The bench starts paused with
      the accepted exploration defaults: 175 acceleration per engine, 250
      one-Light speed, 0.4 extra-speed allowance and 0.8 falloff. Review
      confirmed this directional-force approach works; these defaults carry
      forward into the turning study.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question is whether two aft engines deserve a noticeable speed
      advantage over the angled side pair, beyond their stronger acceleration,
      and how much advantage four engines should gain. Fixed effective mass
      isolates that question; equipment mass, Heavy scaling and free-flight feel
      remain later investigations. Current Fighter turning and the production
      thrust taper remain the baseline.
    </p>
    <p>
      The previous comparison of installed, active and directional counting
      addressed the wrong question and is
      <a href="/lab/studies/archive/ion-ceiling-rules">preserved separately</a>;
      it established no accepted rule.
    </p>

    <h3 id="turning-comparison">02.3 / Turning, release and recovery</h3>
    <p>
      The accepted 175 / 250 / 0.4 / 0.8 defaults carry forward. The same ships
      and input sequence now compare four selectable rotation modes. Free fall
      retains spin; no free fall stops spin when there is no turn command,
      including LR. In both cases turning builds from torque while a single
      button is held. Add stabilizers to examine assisted turns and, under free
      fall with neither button held, automatic counter-spin. Choose a mode and
      Apply &amp; restart. All benches initially remain paused.
    </p>
    <MovementBench
      turning
      label="Turning and stabilizer comparison"
      client:load
    />
    <p>
      Each stabilizer is 0.25 Light strength. The mirrored pair is 85% back from
      the nose and 15% / 85% across the Fighter's full hull bounds. This trial
      now uses the outward-exhaust correction recorded in 02.5, with opposite
      tangential thrust directions: one assists clockwise torque, the other
      counterclockwise torque. Only the appropriate engine fires. That exhaust
      orientation is a study assumption; the accepted placement alone does not
      fix it. Their force affects travel as well as spin. Aft-only fixtures also
      receive the pair in stabilized modes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Angular acceleration uses force × lever arm divided by inertia. The study
      uses a uniform rectangle enclosing the real Fighter hull, measured about
      the existing ship origin, as a shared inertia proxy. Equipment mass
      distribution and production's direct turn-response gain are not included.
      A provisional 120°/s maximum bounds spin in every mode; the input exposes
      it for review. Stabilizers provide physical counter-torque, not passive
      damping; braking tapers on the last substep so it cannot reverse spin.
      They do not auto-brake during LR. With no free fall, release stops spin
      immediately, so stabilizers only assist commanded turns.
    </p>
    <p>
      The artificial side-pair reference instantly sets travel to half its LR
      powered ceiling while either button is held and turns at half the chosen
      maximum rate on a single button. Release preserves its velocity and stops
      its spin immediately. It ignores the rotation-mode selector and
      stabilizers, providing a fixed comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      The third graph shows signed angular speed; positive is clockwise on the
      canvas. The readout shows active stabilizer strength. Watch the one-second
      releases after L and R: does continued spin create useful movement, and
      does counter-thrust bring it back under control? Below one Light
      equivalent, the speed-ceiling curve extends linearly to zero so a
      stabilizer alone has a defined positive ceiling. Existing momentum is
      still never clamped. These are study-local mechanics; no production
      flight, gravity or friction settings change.
    </p>

    <h3 id="stabilizer-comparison">
      02.4 / Stabilizers: help or interference?
    </h3>
    <p>
      Free-fall rotation is accepted: retaining spin is the more interesting
      direction for play. The four-mode trial above is preserved as evidence.
      This comparison fixes free fall and overlays the same Fighter with and
      without stabilizers; both follow the same sequence and tuning. Start with
      the side pair, then check the other engine arrangements if useful.
    </p>
    <MovementBench stabilizers label="Stabilizers on versus off" client:load />
    <p>
      Move the mirrored mounts and rotate their thrust directions in the
      diagram, then Apply &amp; restart. The very same positions and angles
      determine force and torque in the replay. The unassisted path stays fixed
      when only stabilizer geometry changes. Arrows describe force, not exhaust.
      Placement begins at 85% back and 15% / 85% across; the initial angle uses
      the corrected outward-exhaust tangent orientation from 02.5.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep each stabilizer at 0.25 Light. During a commanded turn, the engine
      with the appropriate torque assists; with neither input held, the opposite
      torque slows residual spin. No automatic braking occurs during LR. Moving
      or angling a mount can reduce its leverage to zero or reverse which nozzle
      provides each turn. Braking stops at zero spin. Stabilizer force also
      changes travel, so assess the path and speed as well as the angular-speed
      graph.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question is whether the extra turning authority and recovery improve
      the free-fall experience enough to keep stabilizers. Geometry remains
      experimental; the retained rectangular inertia proxy, fixed effective mass
      and 120°/s default limit are unchanged. These traces support a handling
      hypothesis; a playable trial is still needed to judge control and
      enjoyment.
    </p>

    <h3 id="stabilizer-look">02.5 / Recessed stabilizers on the Fighter</h3>
    <p>
      Orientation correction: L fires the right stabilizer; R fires the left.
      The slits exhaust outward, with force directed oppositely. Effects are
      drawn over the hull and fitted parts in this preview; the production
      renderer still draws plumes underneath and needs that layer-order change
      during integration. The earlier inward-crossing exhaust and same-side
      activation were a misunderstanding, not the selected design. The shared
      placement calculation and current turning trials use this correction;
      changing angles can still reverse torque, which the diagram reports.
    </p>
    <p>
      Selected direction: free-fall rotation, with stabilizers enabled or
      disabled in the ship editor. The next ion defaults are 220 acceleration,
      340 one-Light speed, 0.4 extra-speed allowance, 0.8 falloff and 180°/s
      maximum rotation. Stabilizers remain ion engines at 0.22 Light strength
      each regardless of the main engines. They assist a turn and counter
      residual spin whenever no turn is commanded, including LR. These are the
      next integration targets; the earlier trial configurations remain
      preserved above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Placement and angle now need to work visually as well as mechanically.
      This proposal recesses the stabilizer into a narrow dark slit, exposing
      only a small charcoal engine edge and a blue outlet line. The actual
      production Fighter carries Heavy ions in both side and both aft mounts to
      expose crowding. No editor slot markings appear. The smaller view uses the
      game's hull drawing scale at 1× camera zoom.
    </p>
    <FighterStabilizerLook client:load />
    <p>
      Effects layer over the hull and fitted parts in this preview. Production
      still draws plumes underneath; correcting that layer order belongs in the
      integration plan.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hull, Heavy engines and ion flames use shared rendering. Stabilizer
      slits remain study-only artwork. The selected placement is now 77% back,
      10% / 90% across and 37.7° outward exhaust angle; Reset placement restores
      it. The stationary preview exposes fit and plume overlap, while 02.6
      examines the same appearance through motion and release.
    </p>

    <h3 id="stabilizer-flight">02.6 / The selected placement in flight</h3>
    <p>
      The Fighter placement is selected: 77% back from the nose, 10% / 90%
      across, 37.7° outward exhaust angle. The slit appearance and proposed
      0.22-output flame now run through the same fourteen-second sequence used
      above, with a Heavy ion in each side and aft mount. The placement
      inspector above resets to this reference.
    </p>
    <StabilizerFlight client:load />
    <p>
      This replay uses the shared study motion model with the agreed 220 / 340 /
      0.4 / 0.8 values, 180°/s rotation limit, Heavy strength 1.75 and
      stabilizers 0.22. L fires the right stabilizer; R fires the left. With no
      turn command, including LR, counter-thrust removes residual spin. No
      gravity, energy limit, drag or wrapping is present; rectangular inertia
      and fixed effective mass remain study assumptions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shared Heavy-ion and Light-ion effects emit at the actual nozzles and
      draw over the hull. Emission size follows the delivered combined-force
      fraction; the stabilizer's mark also scales by its 0.22 contribution.
      Existing marks keep their world position, velocity and fade. The
      fixed-orientation camera follows the ship at the selected scale; the grid
      and two-second route trace expose its movement. Playback starts paused.
      Seeking deterministically reconstructs the same 60 Hz flight and emission
      history, with four motion substeps per tick and no randomness.
    </p>

    <h3 id="scout-stabilizers">02.7 / Scout: slits within the swept hull</h3>
    <p>
      Ion characteristics and appearance, including optional Fighter
      stabilizers, are approved. Actual acceleration and speed values remain
      subject to post-study Flight Lab testing. After integration, the Lab
      vessels start with two Light-ion side engines and no aft engines. This is
      an integration target, not a change to current production loadouts.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Scout now needs its own visual placement. Keep the approved recessed
      slit, blue outlet and ion effect; judge their relationship to the Scout's
      round cockpit, swept blades and narrow central keel. The three placement
      buttons offer starting compositions, not accepted positions. Position and
      angle remain adjustable.
    </p>
    <FighterStabilizerLook
      scout
      label="Scout stabilizer visual placement"
      client:load
    />
    <p>
      The preview consumes the real Scout hull and engine mounts. It opens with
      the Light-ion side pair; switch to the full Heavy-ion fit to expose
      crowding around the single aft mount. L and R show all matching engine
      effects with crosswise stabilizer assistance; LR shows all fitted main
      engines. Effects remain above the hull. Percentages use the Scout's own
      hull bounds, not the Fighter's dimensions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Wing roots put the slits on the inner swept surfaces; swept blades move
      them farther aft and outward; central keel tests a tighter placement
      beside the spine. Look for a deliberate recess that belongs to the hull,
      with a clear outlet and no collision with fitted engine bodies. The
      smaller view exposes gameplay-scale readability. This is a visual
      comparison; Scout handling still needs flight testing; the selected
      placement is 67% back, 38% / 62% across, at 67°.
    </p>

    <h3 id="cruiser-stabilizers">
      02.8 / Cruiser: slits along the armored hull
    </h3>
    <p>
      The Scout placement is selected: 67% back, 38% / 62% across and 67°
      outward exhaust angle. Its preview above now opens and resets to those
      values. The cruiser is next: retain the approved slit language while
      finding positions that belong to its long armored hull and clear its three
      aft engines.
    </p>
    <FighterStabilizerLook
      cruiser
      label="Cruiser stabilizer visual placement"
      client:load
    />
    <p>
      Mid flanks, aft shoulders and stern edges provide three starting
      placements. The real cruiser hull, side pair and three aft mounts are
      used. The full Heavy-ion fitting includes two side engines and three aft
      engines to inspect clearance. L and R fire the corresponding main engine
      and crosswise stabilizer; LR fires all installed main engines. Position
      and angle sliders use this hull's own bounds. The selected placement opens
      and resets at 76% back, 23% / 77% across and 63° outward exhaust angle.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="readiness">
    <h2>03 / Ready for an alpha flight trial</h2>
    <p>
      The selected design fits the existing fixed-step simulation, mounted
      engine renderer, ion plume painter and replay tools. Production needs
      persistent angular velocity, a combined directional ion force, automatic
      stabilizer power demand and a saved on/off option. Gravity and other
      engine response families stay outside this study.
    </p>
    <p>
      Free-fall rotation and the recessed slit appearance are accepted. Light
      ion strength is 1; Heavy ion is 1.75 in both force and energy consumption.
      A firing stabilizer provides 0.22 Light-ion thrust and consumes 0.25
      Light-ion energy, independently of the main engine family. L assists with
      the right stabilizer; R with the left. With no turn command, including LR,
      stabilizers oppose spin and stop at zero. Without stabilizers, rotation
      continues after release.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Hull</th>
          <th>Back from nose</th>
          <th>Across</th>
          <th>Outward exhaust</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Fighter</td>
          <td>77%</td>
          <td>10% / 90%</td>
          <td>37.7°</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Scout</td>
          <td>67%</td>
          <td>38% / 62%</td>
          <td>67°</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cruiser</td>
          <td>76%</td>
          <td>23% / 77%</td>
          <td>63°</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The starting rates are 220 acceleration, 340 speed, 0.4 ceiling bonus, 0.8
      falloff and 180°/s rotation cap. Acceleration and speed remain tuning
      hypotheses for the real Flight Lab. Geometry-driven inertia and different
      hull masses will change handling: visual acceptance does not establish the
      balance of Scout or Cruiser.
    </p>
    <p>
      No dependency or gravity change is needed. Alpha adapters use a 115-mass
      reference (Fighter with two Light ions), the study's rectangular hull
      inertia about the ship origin, and built-in stabilizers with no additional
      slot or mass. Existing saves default to off. Mixed builds retain
      Fusion/Plasma pulse, charge and individual speed rules; their ion engines
      and stabilizers use the combined ion rule. These compatibility choices
      need flight review, not another visual-placement study.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="integration">
    <h2>04 / Bring the accepted design into flight</h2>
    <p>
      The integration below is implemented for 0.4.2 in
      <a href="/hangar">the shared builder</a>
      and
      <a href="/hangar">Flight Lab</a>. The preserved 02 examples describe their
      investigation-time conditions; their production-pending statements are
      historical. Post-integration flight balance remains open.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Define validated ion tuning and optional stabilizers in shared
        flight/loadout schemas. Preserve version-2 builds with an off default;
        new builds use two Light-ion side engines.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sum ion force vectors and torque at each simulation substep. Use net
        force for the saturating speed ceiling, retain external overspeed,
        integrate free-fall angular velocity, and cap automatic braking at zero.
        Budget stabilizer energy before applying force.
      </li>
      <li>
        Promote the three accepted slit placements and shared Light-ion flames
        to the build renderer. Draw flight effects above hull and fittings.
        Preserve the exploratory examples as references, including the Heavy-ion
        Fighter and five-engine Cruiser.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add the optional stabilizer switch to builder and Flight Lab. Lab trials
        use the real two-Light-ion loadout, expose the provisional ion values,
        and export/import those settings. Keep scored stock trials separate from
        these unranked experiments.
      </li>
      <li>
        Verify directional summation, Heavy strength, coasting, counter-spin,
        power exhaustion, schema compatibility and deterministic replay. Inspect
        real rendering during L, R, LR and release/fade on each chassis. Run
        repository gates before the authorized 0.4.x release.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The motion reference is 14 one-second inputs: LR, LR, none, L, none, LR,
      none, R, none, R, none, LR, LR, LR; start at rest without randomness. The
      Fighter flight example preserves its follow camera and zoom options.
      Integration checks use the actual game camera and gravity, so trajectories
      are not expected to duplicate the isolated bench.
    </p>
    <p>
      Human flight testing after integration must settle acceleration/speed
      balance and check stabilizer recovery and plume readability on all hulls.
      Automated verification establishes implementation contracts, not that
      these values make the final game fun.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
